{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QKC7bDcrriQwHZDVbHqPey","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t    \"name\": \"020_vision_patch_merger_spatial_shuffle_mlp\",\n3\t    \"hf_id\": \"Qwen/Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Vision patch merger that performs spatial shuffling to merge patches (spatial_merge_size=2), applies layer normalization, and processes through a 2-layer MLP with GELU activation. Converts fine-grained vision tokens into coarser representations by merging 2x2 spatial patches.\",\n5\t    \"axes\": {\n6\t        \"num_patches\": {\n7\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n8\t            \"description\": \"Total number of input patches before merging\"\n9\t        },\n10\t        \"num_merged_patches\": {\n11\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n12\t            \"description\": \"Total number of output patches after merging (num_patches / 4)\"\n13\t        },\n14\t        \"hidden_size\": {\n15\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n16\t            \"value\": 1536\n17\t        },\n18\t        \"spatial_merge_size\": {\n19\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n20\t            \"value\": 2\n21\t        },\n22\t        \"out_hidden_size\": {\n23\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n24\t            \"value\": 3584\n25\t        },\n26\t        \"hidden_size_expanded\": {\n27\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n28\t            \"value\": 6144\n29\t        },\n30\t        \"num_grids\": {\n31\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n32\t            \"description\": \"Number of video/image inputs in the batch\"\n33\t        },\n34\t        \"three\": {\n35\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n36\t            \"value\": 3\n37\t        }\n38\t    },\n39\t    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": \"get_inputs\",\n40\t    \"inputs\": {\n41\t        \"hidden\": {\n42\t            \"shape\": [\n43\t                \"num_patches\",\n44\t                \"hidden_size\"\n45\t            ],\n46\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n47\t            \"description\": \"Input hidden states from vision encoder\"\n48\t        },\n49\t        \"grid_thw\": {\n50\t            \"shape\": [\n51\t                \"num_grids\",\n52\t                \"three\"\n53\t            ],\n54\t            \"dtype\": \"int64\",\n55\t            \"description\": \"Grid dimensions [T, H, W] for each input\"\n56\t        },\n57\t        \"ln_weight\": {\n58\t            \"shape\": [\n59\t                \"hidden_size\"\n60\t            ],\n61\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n62\t            \"description\": \"Layer normalization weight\"\n63\t        },\n64\t        \"ln_bias\": {\n65\t            \"shape\": [\n66\t                \"hidden_size\"\n67\t            ],\n68\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n69\t            \"description\": \"Layer normalization bias\"\n70\t        },\n71\t        \"fc1_weight\": {\n72\t            \"shape\": [\n73\t                \"hidden_size_expanded\",\n74\t                \"hidden_size_expanded\"\n75\t            ],\n76\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n77\t            \"description\": \"First MLP layer weight\"\n78\t        },\n79\t        \"fc1_bias\": {\n80\t            \"shape\": [\n81\t                \"hidden_size_expanded\"\n82\t            ],\n83\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n84\t            \"description\": \"First MLP layer bias\"\n85\t        },\n86\t        \"fc2_weight\": {\n87\t            \"shape\": [\n88\t                \"out_hidden_size\",\n89\t                \"hidden_size_expanded\"\n90\t            ],\n91\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n92\t            \"description\": \"Second MLP layer weight\"\n93\t        },\n94\t        \"fc2_bias\": {\n95\t            \"shape\": [\n96\t                \"out_hidden_size\"\n97\t            ],\n98\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n99\t            \"description\": \"Second MLP layer bias\"\n100\t        },\n101\t        \"eps\": {\n102\t            \"shape\": null,\n103\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n104\t            \"description\": \"Layer normalization epsilon\"\n105\t        }\n106\t    },\n107\t    \"outputs\": {\n108\t        \"output\": {\n109\t            \"shape\": [\n110\t                \"num_merged_patches\",\n111\t                \"out_hidden_size\"\n112\t            ],\n113\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n114\t            \"description\": \"Output hidden states after merging and MLP\"\n115\t        }\n116\t    },\n117\t    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport math\\n\\ndef get_inputs(axes_and_scalars: dict, device: torch.device) -> dict[str, torch.Tensor]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Generate inputs with valid grid_thw that matches num_patches.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    num_patches = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_patches\\\"]\\n    num_merged_patches = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_merged_patches\\\"]\\n    num_grids = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_grids\\\"]\\n    hidden_size = 1536\\n    hidden_size_expanded = 6144\\n    out_hidden_size = 3584\\n    merge_size = 2\\n    eps = 1e-6\\n    \\n    # Generate grid_thw such that total patches matches num_patches\\n    # Each grid contributes T * H * W patches, and H, W must be divisible by merge_size\\n    patches_per_grid = num_patches // num_grids\\n    \\n    # Find valid H, W that are divisible by merge_size\\n    # We want T * H * W = patches_per_grid\\n    # Let's use T=1 for simplicity and find H, W\\n    sqrt_patches = int(math.sqrt(patches_per_grid))\\n    # Round to nearest multiple of merge_size\\n    h = (sqrt_patches // merge_size) * merge_size\\n    if h == 0:\\n        h = merge_size\\n    w = (patches_per_grid // h // merge_size) * merge_size\\n    if w == 0:\\n        w = merge_size\\n    t = patches_per_grid // (h * w)\\n    if t == 0:\\n        t = 1\\n    \\n    # Adjust to match exactly\\n    actual_patches_per_grid = t * h * w\\n    \\n    # Create grid_thw tensor\\n    grid_thw = torch.zeros((num_grids, 3), dtype=torch.int64, device=device)\\n    remaining_patches = num_patches\\n    for i in range(num_grids):\\n        if i == num_grids - 1:\\n            # Last grid takes remaining patches\\n            patches_for_this = remaining_patches\\n        else:\\n            patches_for_this = actual_patches_per_grid\\n        \\n        # Find valid T, H, W for this grid\\n        sqrt_p = int(math.sqrt(patches_for_this))\\n        h_i = (sqrt_p // merge_size) * merge_size\\n        if h_i == 0:\\n            h_i = merge_size\\n        w_i = (patches_for_this // h_i // merge_size) * merge_size\\n        if w_i == 0:\\n            w_i = merge_size\\n        t_i = patches_for_this // (h_i * w_i)\\n        if t_i == 0:\\n            t_i = 1\\n        \\n        grid_thw[i, 0] = t_i\\n        grid_thw[i, 1] = h_i\\n        grid_thw[i, 2] = w_i\\n        remaining_patches -= t_i * h_i * w_i\\n    \\n    hidden = torch.randn(num_patches, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    ln_weight = torch.ones(hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    ln_bias = torch.zeros(hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    fc1_weight = torch.randn(hidden_size_expanded, hidden_size_expanded, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) / math.sqrt(hidden_size_expanded)\\n    fc1_bias = torch.randn(hidden_size_expanded, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    fc2_weight = torch.randn(out_hidden_size, hidden_size_expanded, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) / math.sqrt(hidden_size_expanded)\\n    fc2_bias = torch.randn(out_hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    \\n    return {\\n        \\\"hidden\\\": hidden,\\n        \\\"grid_thw\\\": grid_thw,\\n        \\\"ln_weight\\\": ln_weight,\\n        \\\"ln_bias\\\": ln_bias,\\n        \\\"fc1_weight\\\": fc1_weight,\\n        \\\"fc1_bias\\\": fc1_bias,\\n        \\\"fc2_weight\\\": fc2_weight,\\n        \\\"fc2_bias\\\": fc2_bias,\\n        \\\"eps\\\": eps,\\n    }\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden: torch.Tensor,\\n    grid_thw: torch.Tensor,\\n    ln_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    ln_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    fc1_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    fc1_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    fc2_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    fc2_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    eps: float,\\n):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Vision patch merger with spatial shuffling and MLP.\\n    \\n    1. Apply layer normalization (pre-shuffle)\\n    2. Spatial shuffle to merge 2x2 patches\\n    3. Two-layer MLP with GELU activation\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    merge_size = 2\\n    hidden_size = 1536\\n    hidden_size_expanded = 6144\\n    \\n    # Step 1: Layer normalization (pre-shuffle, on hidden_size dimension)\\n    hidden_fp32 = hidden.to(torch.float32)\\n    mean = hidden_fp32.mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\\n    var = hidden_fp32.var(dim=-1, keepdim=True, unbiased=False)\\n    hidden_norm = (hidden_fp32 - mean) / torch.sqrt(var + eps)\\n    hidden_norm = hidden_norm * ln_weight.to(torch.float32) + ln_bias.to(torch.float32)\\n    hidden_norm = hidden_norm.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    # Step 2: Spatial shuffle to merge patches\\n    shuffled_patches = []\\n    offset = 0\\n    \\n    for i in range(grid_thw.shape[0]):\\n        t = grid_thw[i, 0].item()\\n        h = grid_thw[i, 1].item()\\n        w = grid_thw[i, 2].item()\\n        \\n        num_patches_this = t * h * w\\n        patches = hidden_norm[offset:offset + num_patches_this]\\n        \\n        h_merged = h // merge_size\\n        w_merged = w // merge_size\\n        \\n        # Reshape to (T, H/merge_size, merge_size, W/merge_size, merge_size, C)\\n        patches = patches.view(t, h_merged, merge_size, w_merged, merge_size, hidden_size)\\n        \\n        # Permute to (T, H/merge_size, W/merge_size, merge_size, merge_size, C)\\n        patches = patches.permute(0, 1, 3, 2, 4, 5)\\n        \\n        # Flatten spatial merge groups: (T * H/merge_size * W/merge_size, merge_size^2 * C)\\n        patches = patches.reshape(t * h_merged * w_merged, hidden_size_expanded)\\n        \\n        shuffled_patches.append(patches)\\n        offset += num_patches_this\\n    \\n    hidden_shuffled = torch.cat(shuffled_patches, dim=0)\\n    \\n    # Step 3: Two-layer MLP with GELU\\n    # FC1: (num_merged_patches, hidden_size_expanded) @ (hidden_size_expanded, hidden_size_expanded).T\\n    hidden_fc1 = torch.nn.functional.linear(hidden_shuffled, fc1_weight, fc1_bias)\\n    \\n    # GELU activation\\n    hidden_gelu = torch.nn.functional.gelu(hidden_fc1)\\n    \\n    # FC2: (num_merged_patches, hidden_size_expanded) @ (out_hidden_size, hidden_size_expanded).T\\n    output = torch.nn.functional.linear(hidden_gelu, fc2_weight, fc2_bias)\\n    \\n    return output\\n\"\n118\t}\n119\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1aeee9ce-f5a1-4307-8fcf-92628a42d73f","uuid":"0108b1ae-796c-4c32-bd59-53111f2da8f7","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:10:30.383Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-020_vision_patch_merger_spatial_shuffle_mlp/task/definition.json","content":"{\n    \"name\": \"020_vision_patch_merger_spatial_shuffle_mlp\",\n    \"hf_id\": \"Qwen/Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct\",\n    \"description\": \"Vision patch merger that performs spatial shuffling to merge patches (spatial_merge_size=2), applies layer normalization, and processes through a 2-layer MLP with GELU activation. Converts fine-grained vision tokens into coarser representations by merging 2x2 spatial patches.\",\n    \"axes\": {\n        \"num_patches\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Total number of input patches before merging\"\n        },\n        \"num_merged_patches\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Total number of output patches after merging (num_patches / 4)\"\n        },\n        \"hidden_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 1536\n        },\n        \"spatial_merge_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 2\n        },\n        \"out_hidden_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 3584\n        },\n        \"hidden_size_expanded\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 6144\n        },\n        \"num_grids\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Number of video/image inputs in the batch\"\n        },\n        \"three\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 3\n        }\n    },\n    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": \"get_inputs\",\n    \"inputs\": {\n        \"hidden\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_patches\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Input hidden states from vision encoder\"\n        },\n        \"grid_thw\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_grids\",\n                \"three\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"int64\",\n            \"description\": \"Grid dimensions [T, H, W] for each input\"\n        },\n        \"ln_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Layer normalization weight\"\n        },\n        \"ln_bias\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Layer normalization bias\"\n        },\n        \"fc1_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size_expanded\",\n                \"hidden_size_expanded\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"First MLP layer weight\"\n        },\n        \"fc1_bias\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size_expanded\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"First MLP layer bias\"\n        },\n        \"fc2_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"out_hidden_size\",\n                \"hidden_size_expanded\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Second MLP layer weight\"\n        },\n        \"fc2_bias\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"out_hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Second MLP layer bias\"\n        },\n        \"eps\": {\n            \"shape\": null,\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Layer normalization epsilon\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"outputs\": {\n        \"output\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_merged_patches\",\n                \"out_hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Output hidden states after merging and MLP\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport math\\n\\ndef get_inputs(axes_and_scalars: dict, device: torch.device) -> dict[str, torch.Tensor]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Generate inputs with valid grid_thw that matches num_patches.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    num_patches = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_patches\\\"]\\n    num_merged_patches = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_merged_patches\\\"]\\n    num_grids = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_grids\\\"]\\n    hidden_size = 1536\\n    hidden_size_expanded = 6144\\n    out_hidden_size = 3584\\n    merge_size = 2\\n    eps = 1e-6\\n    \\n    # Generate grid_thw such that total patches matches num_patches\\n    # Each grid contributes T * H * W patches, and H, W must be divisible by merge_size\\n    patches_per_grid = num_patches // num_grids\\n    \\n    # Find valid H, W that are divisible by merge_size\\n    # We want T * H * W = patches_per_grid\\n    # Let's use T=1 for simplicity and find H, W\\n    sqrt_patches = int(math.sqrt(patches_per_grid))\\n    # Round to nearest multiple of merge_size\\n    h = (sqrt_patches // merge_size) * merge_size\\n    if h == 0:\\n        h = merge_size\\n    w = (patches_per_grid // h // merge_size) * merge_size\\n    if w == 0:\\n        w = merge_size\\n    t = patches_per_grid // (h * w)\\n    if t == 0:\\n        t = 1\\n    \\n    # Adjust to match exactly\\n    actual_patches_per_grid = t * h * w\\n    \\n    # Create grid_thw tensor\\n    grid_thw = torch.zeros((num_grids, 3), dtype=torch.int64, device=device)\\n    remaining_patches = num_patches\\n    for i in range(num_grids):\\n        if i == num_grids - 1:\\n            # Last grid takes remaining patches\\n            patches_for_this = remaining_patches\\n        else:\\n            patches_for_this = actual_patches_per_grid\\n        \\n        # Find valid T, H, W for this grid\\n        sqrt_p = int(math.sqrt(patches_for_this))\\n        h_i = (sqrt_p // merge_size) * merge_size\\n        if h_i == 0:\\n            h_i = merge_size\\n        w_i = (patches_for_this // h_i // merge_size) * merge_size\\n        if w_i == 0:\\n            w_i = merge_size\\n        t_i = patches_for_this // (h_i * w_i)\\n        if t_i == 0:\\n            t_i = 1\\n        \\n        grid_thw[i, 0] = t_i\\n        grid_thw[i, 1] = h_i\\n        grid_thw[i, 2] = w_i\\n        remaining_patches -= t_i * h_i * w_i\\n    \\n    hidden = torch.randn(num_patches, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    ln_weight = torch.ones(hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    ln_bias = torch.zeros(hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    fc1_weight = torch.randn(hidden_size_expanded, hidden_size_expanded, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) / math.sqrt(hidden_size_expanded)\\n    fc1_bias = torch.randn(hidden_size_expanded, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    fc2_weight = torch.randn(out_hidden_size, hidden_size_expanded, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) / math.sqrt(hidden_size_expanded)\\n    fc2_bias = torch.randn(out_hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    \\n    return {\\n        \\\"hidden\\\": hidden,\\n        \\\"grid_thw\\\": grid_thw,\\n        \\\"ln_weight\\\": ln_weight,\\n        \\\"ln_bias\\\": ln_bias,\\n        \\\"fc1_weight\\\": fc1_weight,\\n        \\\"fc1_bias\\\": fc1_bias,\\n        \\\"fc2_weight\\\": fc2_weight,\\n        \\\"fc2_bias\\\": fc2_bias,\\n        \\\"eps\\\": eps,\\n    }\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden: torch.Tensor,\\n    grid_thw: torch.Tensor,\\n    ln_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    ln_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    fc1_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    fc1_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    fc2_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    fc2_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    eps: float,\\n):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Vision patch merger with spatial shuffling and MLP.\\n    \\n    1. Apply layer normalization (pre-shuffle)\\n    2. Spatial shuffle to merge 2x2 patches\\n    3. Two-layer MLP with GELU activation\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    merge_size = 2\\n    hidden_size = 1536\\n    hidden_size_expanded = 6144\\n    \\n    # Step 1: Layer normalization (pre-shuffle, on hidden_size dimension)\\n    hidden_fp32 = hidden.to(torch.float32)\\n    mean = hidden_fp32.mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\\n    var = hidden_fp32.var(dim=-1, keepdim=True, unbiased=False)\\n    hidden_norm = (hidden_fp32 - mean) / torch.sqrt(var + eps)\\n    hidden_norm = hidden_norm * ln_weight.to(torch.float32) + ln_bias.to(torch.float32)\\n    hidden_norm = hidden_norm.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    # Step 2: Spatial shuffle to merge patches\\n    shuffled_patches = []\\n    offset = 0\\n    \\n    for i in range(grid_thw.shape[0]):\\n        t = grid_thw[i, 0].item()\\n        h = grid_thw[i, 1].item()\\n        w = grid_thw[i, 2].item()\\n        \\n        num_patches_this = t * h * w\\n        patches = hidden_norm[offset:offset + num_patches_this]\\n        \\n        h_merged = h // merge_size\\n        w_merged = w // merge_size\\n        \\n        # Reshape to (T, H/merge_size, merge_size, W/merge_size, merge_size, C)\\n        patches = patches.view(t, h_merged, merge_size, w_merged, merge_size, hidden_size)\\n        \\n        # Permute to (T, H/merge_size, W/merge_size, merge_size, merge_size, C)\\n        patches = patches.permute(0, 1, 3, 2, 4, 5)\\n        \\n        # Flatten spatial merge groups: (T * H/merge_size * W/merge_size, merge_size^2 * C)\\n        patches = patches.reshape(t * h_merged * w_merged, hidden_size_expanded)\\n        \\n        shuffled_patches.append(patches)\\n        offset += num_patches_this\\n    \\n    hidden_shuffled = torch.cat(shuffled_patches, dim=0)\\n    \\n    # Step 3: Two-layer MLP with GELU\\n    # FC1: (num_merged_patches, hidden_size_expanded) @ (hidden_size_expanded, hidden_size_expanded).T\\n    hidden_fc1 = torch.nn.functional.linear(hidden_shuffled, fc1_weight, fc1_bias)\\n    \\n    # GELU activation\\n    hidden_gelu = torch.nn.functional.gelu(hidden_fc1)\\n    \\n    # FC2: (num_merged_patches, hidden_size_expanded) @ (out_hidden_size, hidden_size_expanded).T\\n    output = torch.nn.functional.linear(hidden_gelu, fc2_weight, fc2_bias)\\n    \\n    return output\\n\"\n}\n","numLines":119,"startLine":1,"totalLines":119}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01URrApzNc7uKtTMy3MEEDCV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L1/020 Vision Patch Merger (Spatial Shuffle + LayerNorm + 2-layer GELU MLP)\n2\t\n3\tTarget device: **NVIDIA A800 (sm_80, Ampere)**. Framework: Triton primary compute,\n4\tPyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext computational fallback.\n5\t\n6\t## 1. Operation semantics (from `task/definition.json` reference)\n7\t\n8\tSignature:\n9\t```\n10\trun(hidden[num_patches,1536] bf16,\n11\t    grid_thw[num_grids,3] int64,\n12\t    ln_weight[1536] bf16, ln_bias[1536] bf16,\n13\t    fc1_weight[6144,6144] bf16, fc1_bias[6144] bf16,\n14\t    fc2_weight[3584,6144] bf16, fc2_bias[3584] bf16,\n15\t    eps: float32) -> output[num_merged,3584] bf16\n16\t```\n17\tConstants: `hidden_size C = 1536`, `merge_size = 2`, `hidden_size_expanded E = 6144 = 4*C`,\n18\t`out_hidden_size O = 3584`, `num_merged = num_patches / 4`.\n19\t\n20\tReference pipeline (semantically):\n21\t\n22\t1. **LayerNorm (pre-shuffle), computed in fp32, per input patch over the C=1536 axis**\n23\t   - `x = hidden.float()`\n24\t   - `mean = x.mean(-1, keepdim)`, `var = x.var(-1, keepdim, unbiased=False)` (÷N, N=1536)\n25\t   - `xhat = (x - mean) / sqrt(var + eps)`\n26\t   - `y = xhat * ln_weight.float() + ln_bias.float()`  → cast to **bf16** → `hidden_norm[num_patches,1536]`\n27\t   - Note: generator fills `ln_weight=ones`, `ln_bias=zeros` (identity affine), but we implement\n28\t     the **general** affine using the passed tensors (do not hardcode).\n29\t\n30\t2. **Spatial shuffle (2×2 patch merge), per grid `(t,h,w)`** (h,w even by construction):\n31\t   - `view(t, h/2, 2, w/2, 2, C)` → `permute(0,1,3,2,4,5)` → `reshape(t*(h/2)*(w/2), 4*C=E)`.\n32\t   - Grids concatenated in order → `hidden_shuffled[num_merged, 6144]` bf16.\n33\t   - Decoding: for a grid with row-major patch index `p = ((tt*h + hh)*w + ww)`,\n34\t     with `hh = hm*2 + a`, `ww = wm*2 + b`:\n35\t     - output row (within grid) = `(tt*(h/2) + hm)*(w/2) + wm`\n36\t     - within-row **block** `β = a*2 + b ∈ {0,1,2,3}` occupies columns `[β*1536 : (β+1)*1536]`.\n37\t     - i.e. the 6144-vector = `concat( patch(a=0,b=0), patch(a=0,b=1), patch(a=1,b=0), patch(a=1,b=1) )`.\n38\t   - This is a **pure row-gather / permutation** of already-LayerNormed patches; LayerNorm is\n39\t     per-patch over C so it commutes with the shuffle (LN then gather == gather then LN).\n40\t\n41\t3. **FC1**: `linear(A, fc1_weight, fc1_bias)` = `A @ fc1_weight^T + fc1_bias`,\n42\t   `[num_merged,6144] @ [6144,6144]^T → [num_merged,6144]`. cuBLAS returns **bf16**.\n43\t\n44\t4. **GELU exact (erf)**: `F.gelu(..., approximate='none')` on the bf16 FC1 output → bf16.\n45\t\n46\t5. **FC2**: `linear(gelu, fc2_weight, fc2_bias)` = `[num_merged,6144] @ [3584,6144]^T + bias\n47\t   → [num_merged,3584]` bf16 = **output**.\n48\t\n49\tKey structural fact: `fc1_weight` columns are indexed by the merged 6144 axis, so\n50\t`FC1[m] = Σ_{β=0..3} ( LN(patch_β) @ fc1_weight[:, β*1536:(β+1)*1536]^T ) + bias`. The permutation\n51\tis over **rows/patches**, not columns, so it cannot be folded into the static weight — an explicit\n52\tgather is required.\n53\t\n54\t## 2. Feedback workloads and derived quantities\n55\t\n56\t| uuid# | num_patches | num_merged (M) | num_grids | atol | rtol |\n57\t|------:|------------:|---------------:|----------:|-----:|-----:|\n58\t| 1 | 2048 | 512 | 2 | 0.0022 | 0.05 |\n59\t| 2 | 6400 | 1600 | 1 | 0.0020 | 0.05 |\n60\t| 3 | 1024 | 256 | 4 | 0.0014 | 0.05 |\n61\t| 4 | 512  | 128 | 2 | 0.0021 | 0.05 |\n62\t| 5 | 4096 | 1024 | 4 | 0.0026 | 0.05 |\n63\t\n64\tM ∈ {512, 1600, 256, 128, 1024} — all divisible by 128 except **256/128 boundaries fine**;\n65\t128 and 256 are the small, memory-bound cases. All M divisible by 64; 1600 divisible by 64 not 128\n66\t(1600 = 25*64 = 12.5*128) → a BLOCK_M of 128 leaves a half tile, 64 tiles evenly. Choose GEMM\n67\tblock/masking that handles arbitrary M (mask the M tail) rather than assuming divisibility.\n68\t\n69\tCompute / traffic estimates (per call):\n70\t- FC1 flops = `2*M*E*E = M*75.5e6`; FC2 flops = `2*M*E*O = M*44.0e6`.\n71\t  - M=1600: FC1 ≈ 120.8 GFLOP, FC2 ≈ 70.5 GFLOP, total ≈ 191 GFLOP.\n72\t  - M=128: total ≈ 15.3 GFLOP.\n73\t- Weight bytes (read at least once): fc1 = 6144*6144*2 = 75.5 MB, fc2 = 3584*6144*2 = 44.0 MB;\n74\t  combined ≈ 119.5 MB.\n75\t- Roofline (A800 ~312 TFLOPS bf16, HBM ~1.9 TB/s): weights dominate for **M ≲ ~200**\n76\t  (M=128 ≈ 63 µs weight-read vs ≈ 49 µs compute → memory-bound); M=1600 is compute-bound\n77\t  (~0.6 ms ideal). The two small workloads (M=128, 256) are where the reference's **host-side\n78\t  overhead** (Python per-grid loop with `.item()` syncs + list-append + `torch.cat`) is most\n79\t  visible and where fusion wins the most.\n80\t\n81\t## 3. Where the speedup comes from (baseline inefficiencies)\n82\t\n83\tThe reference `run` is timed as the baseline. Its overheads over an optimal GPU pipeline:\n84\t1. **Host-side grid loop with `grid_thw[i,j].item()`** → up to `3*num_grids` device syncs per call,\n85\t   plus Python-level slicing, `permute/reshape` (materializing copies), list build, and `torch.cat`\n86\t   (an extra full read+write of the shuffled A matrix).\n87\t2. **fp32 LayerNorm materialization**: `hidden.float()` allocates a full fp32 copy of\n88\t   `[num_patches,1536]` and multiple intermediate tensors.\n89\t3. **Separate kernels** for LN, cast, cat, two GEMMs, bias adds, and GELU (each a launch + full\n90\t   HBM round trip of the activations).\n91\t\n92\tOur plan removes host syncs (indices computed from `grid_thw` once), fuses LN+shuffle into a single\n93\tkernel, fuses bias+GELU into the FC1 epilogue and bias into the FC2 epilogue, and keeps activations\n94\tin bf16. The two GEMMs are the irreducible cost; the win is (a) eliminating overhead/extra passes\n95\tand (b) matching cuBLAS GEMM throughput as closely as possible with autotuned Triton.\n96\t\n97\t**Risk:** cuBLAS bf16 GEMM on A800 is strong; for the compute-bound M=1600 case a Triton GEMM may\n98\tland at ~0.8–1.0× of cuBLAS. Overall geomean depends on winning big on small M (overhead-dominated)\n99\twhile staying near-parity on large M. This is the central tradeoff to validate empirically.\n100\t\n101\t## 4. Triton design space\n102\t\n103\t### 4.1 LayerNorm + shuffle kernel (K1)\n104\tProduces `A_shuffled[num_merged, 6144]` bf16 directly (fused LN + gather), avoiding a separate cast\n105\tand `torch.cat`.\n106\t- **Layout choice A (one program per output patch)** — preferred: grid = `num_merged`. Each program\n107\t  loops β=0..3, gathers source patch row `src(m,β)`, loads its 1536 bf16 values, casts fp32, computes\n108\t  mean/var over 1536, applies `xhat*w+b`, stores 1536 bf16 contiguously at columns `[β*1536:(β+1)*1536]`.\n109\t  Writes are fully contiguous (coalesced 6144-wide row); 4 gathered strided loads. Per-program working\n110\t  set ~1536 fp32 = 6 KB (loop β, don't hold all 4 at once).\n111\t- **Layout choice B (one program per input patch, scatter)**: grid = `num_patches`; each normalizes one\n112\t  patch and scatters 1536 to its computed `(out_row, β)` slot. Same traffic; store is contiguous 1536.\n113\t  Slightly simpler indexing but requires input→dest map. Either is fine; pick A for contiguous 6144\n114\t  stores and simpler reduction.\n115\t- Reduction over C=1536: `1536 = 3*512`. Use `BLOCK_C` covering 1536 with masking, or a fixed 1536\n116\t  `tl.arange` (power-of-two pad to 2048 with mask). Two-pass (mean, then var) or one-pass\n117\t  Welford/`sum`+`sum(x^2)`; a single load into registers + `sum`/`sum(x*x)` is simplest and exact in fp32.\n118\t- eps added inside sqrt: `rstd = 1/sqrt(var + eps)`.\n119\t\n120\t### 4.2 Index construction (avoid host sync)\n121\tTwo options; both are \"plumbing\":\n122\t- **A (single small transfer)**: `grid_thw.to('cpu')` once (4×3 int64) → compute prefix sums and a\n123\t  gather-index int32 tensor `src_idx[num_merged, 4]` (or a base+arithmetic form) on host, upload once.\n124\t  One tiny sync — vastly cheaper than the reference's per-grid `.item()` loop. Simple, robust for\n125\t  `num_grids ∈ {1,2,4}`.\n126\t- **B (fully on-GPU)**: a trivial index kernel reads `grid_thw` on device, does a sequential scan over\n127\t  `num_grids (≤4)`, writes `src_idx`. Zero host sync. More code; keep as a later refinement if the\n128\t  single transfer shows up in timing.\n129\t- `num_merged` and the GEMM launch dims need **no** sync: `num_merged = hidden.shape[0] // 4` is known\n130\t  from tensor shape. Only the permutation mapping depends on `grid_thw` values.\n131\t- Correctness note: implement fully general `(t,h,w)` with h,w even; Σ over grids of `t*(h/2)*(w/2)`\n132\t  = num_patches/4 = num_merged by construction. Do not assume a single grid or square grids.\n133\t\n134\t### 4.3 FC1 GEMM (K2): `A[M,6144] @ fc1_weight[6144,6144]^T + bias`, GELU epilogue\n135\t- Standard tiled bf16 GEMM, **fp32 accumulator** (matches cuBLAS accumulation).\n136\t- Weight is row-major `[N=out, K=in]`; accessing `W[n, k]` gives K contiguous along the K reduction —\n137\t  natural for `tl.dot(a[BM,BK], w[BK,BN])` after loading `W` tile as `[BK,BN]` via transpose indexing.\n138\t- Epilogue: `acc += fc1_bias[n]`; **match reference rounding**: reference rounds FC1 to bf16 *before*\n139\t  GELU (cuBLAS bf16 output), so cast `acc` (with bias) to bf16, then apply GELU, then keep bf16 for FC2\n140\t  input. (Applying GELU on fp32 then rounding differs by < bf16 ulp; will test both, prefer the\n141\t  reference-matching order to be safe against the tight atol.)\n142\t- **GELU exact**: `0.5*x*(1 + erf(x * 0.70710678118654752440))` using `tl.math.erf` (or libdevice\n143\t  erf). **Do not** use tanh approximation unless testing shows it stays within atol — erf is the\n144\t  reference and safer given atol as low as 0.0014.\n145\t- Autotune space: `BLOCK_M ∈ {64,128}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32,64}`,\n146\t  `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {3,4,5}`, `GROUP_M ∈ {4,8}` (L2 grouping / swizzle).\n147\t- **Occupancy for small M**: M=128 with BLOCK_M=128 → 1 M-tile; N=6144/BLOCK_N tiles. With BLOCK_N=128\n148\t  → 48 programs on 108 SMs (under-utilized). Mitigations: smaller BLOCK_M/BLOCK_N to raise tile count,\n149\t  or **split-K** (2–4 way) to fill SMs (needs fp32 atomic add or a second reduction pass). Consider a\n150\t  separate small-M config selected by M.\n151\t\n152\t### 4.4 FC2 GEMM (K3): `gelu[M,6144] @ fc2_weight[3584,6144]^T + bias`\n153\t- Same GEMM template, `K=6144`, `N=3584`, bias epilogue only, output bf16.\n154\t- `3584 = 28*128 = 14*256` → divisible by 128 and 256; N tiling clean.\n155\t- Same autotune space; N smaller so fewer N-tiles → split-K more relevant for small M.\n156\t\n157\t### 4.5 Fusion boundaries\n158\t- Cannot fuse FC1 and FC2 into one kernel (GELU nonlinearity + different K/N reductions). Keep 3\n159\t  kernels: K1 (LN+shuffle), K2 (FC1+bias+GELU), K3 (FC2+bias).\n160\t- Optional later: skip materializing `A_shuffled` and gather normalized rows inside the FC1 prologue.\n161\t  Blocked by LN needing the whole 1536 row to compute mean/var (a BLOCK_K < 1536 tile lacks the full\n162\t  row), and 1536 not being a power of two. Simpler and nearly-free to materialize A in K1 first\n163\t  (extra ≈ M*6144*2 bytes write+read; ≈ 19.6 MB at M=1600 vs 191 GFLOP — negligible). Prefer\n164\t  materialize-then-GEMM.\n165\t\n166\t## 5. Numerical correctness & risks\n167\t\n168\t1. **LayerNorm precision**: accumulate mean/var in fp32, `unbiased=False` (÷1536), affine in fp32,\n169\t   cast output to bf16 — exactly mirrors the reference. Risk: using bf16 accumulation would blow atol.\n170\t2. **GEMM accumulation**: fp32 accumulator required to match cuBLAS. bf16 inputs.\n171\t3. **GELU form**: exact erf. tanh-approx GELU differs by up to ~3e-4 in activation; after the FC2 sum\n172\t   over 6144 terms it could approach the atol (0.0014–0.0026). Use erf; keep tanh only as a tunable to\n173\t   test if it ever helps speed without failing tolerance.\n174\t4. **Intermediate rounding order (FC1→GELU)**: reference rounds to bf16 before GELU. Replicate to avoid\n175\t   a systematic bias near the tight atol.\n176\t5. **Output dtype**: bf16 store with round-to-nearest (Triton default).\n177\t6. **Value magnitudes**: LN output ~unit variance; FC1/FC2 outputs O(1) (weights scaled by\n178\t   1/sqrt(6144), bias N(0,1)). atol ~0.002 on O(1) values ≈ 0.2% absolute; bf16 has ~0.4% relative\n179\t   precision but fp32-accumulated GEMM keeps error well inside the combined `atol + rtol*|ref|`\n180\t   (rtol 0.05 dominates for |ref|≳0.04). Expect comfortable pass with fp32 accum + erf.\n181\t7. **Determinism**: no split-K atomics in the correctness-first candidate (atomic fp32 add reorders\n182\t   sums → small nondeterminism); if split-K is adopted for speed, re-verify tolerance.\n183\t8. **Edge/general handling**: arbitrary `num_grids ∈ {1,2,4}`, arbitrary even (h,w), t≥1; M-tail masking\n184\t   in GEMM (M not always a multiple of BLOCK_M, e.g. 1600 vs 128).\n185\t\n186\t## 6. Validation strategy\n187\t\n188\tConstraints: I cannot run CUDA, a profiler, or any harness directly; the **only** correctness+timing\n189\tsignal is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` over the fixed 5 workloads (one candidate =\n190\tall 5). Budget: 100 candidate evals; token soft/hard 1.0M/1.2M.\n191\t\n192\tPlan:\n193\t- **c001 — correctness-first, conservative**: K1 LN+shuffle (per-output-patch, fp32 LN), K2 FC1 GEMM\n194\t  (moderate config e.g. BM128/BN128/BK64, warps8, stages3, fp32 accum, bias, erf GELU with\n195\t  bf16-before-GELU rounding), K3 FC2 GEMM (bias). Host-side index build (option A). Evaluate; confirm\n196\t  all 5 pass and record per-workload speedup + geomean as the anchor.\n197\t- **Iterate one dimension per candidate** (each a new immutable ID):\n198\t  - GEMM autotune configs (block sizes, stages, warps, GROUP_M/swizzle).\n199\t  - Small-M path (split-K or smaller blocks) for M=128/256; verify tolerance still holds after any\n200\t    atomic-based split-K.\n201\t  - K1 layout / vectorization tweaks; on-GPU index kernel (option B) if the single transfer shows cost.\n202\t  - Epilogue rounding-order A/B test; erf vs tanh (only if within tolerance).\n203\t- **Guardrails**: never regress a passing candidate's correctness for speed; a Triton failure is\n204\t  invalid (no Torch fallback). Track cumulative eval count; stop when geomean converges, then write\n205\t  `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Never run `final` without operator approval.\n206\t- Record for every candidate in `candidates.jsonl`: parent, source hash, hypothesis, validation,\n207\t  per-workload result, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage.\n208\t\n209\t## 7. Skill usage\n210\t\n211\t- `KernelWiki`: **not applicable** — Blackwell/Hopper only; target is A800/sm_80 (Ampere).\n212\t- `ncu-report-skill`: **not applicable / not permitted** — B200/sm_100 and requires running a profiler,\n213\t  which the isolation rules forbid.\n214\t- No other external knowledge sources are permitted; optimization relies on standard Ampere Triton\n215\t  GEMM practice and the evaluator feedback loop.\n216\t\n217\t## 8. Open questions to resolve empirically via the evaluator\n218\t\n219\t1. Does Triton FC1/FC2 reach cuBLAS parity at M=1600 (compute-bound)? If not, how large is the deficit\n220\t   vs the overhead win on small M in the geomean?\n221\t2. Is the single `grid_thw` host transfer negligible, or worth the on-GPU index kernel?\n222\t3. Best small-M strategy (split-K vs small blocks) and whether split-K stays within atol.\n223\t4. Does the bf16-before-GELU rounding order matter for tolerance vs a pure-fp32 epilogue?\n224\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1aeee9ce-f5a1-4307-8fcf-92628a42d73f","uuid":"506f2875-8f4b-4ada-a024-149337a71362","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:10:30.384Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-020_vision_patch_merger_spatial_shuffle_mlp/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L1/020 Vision Patch Merger (Spatial Shuffle + LayerNorm + 2-layer GELU MLP)\n\nTarget device: **NVIDIA A800 (sm_80, Ampere)**. Framework: Triton primary compute,\nPyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext computational fallback.\n\n## 1. Operation semantics (from `task/definition.json` reference)\n\nSignature:\n```\nrun(hidden[num_patches,1536] bf16,\n    grid_thw[num_grids,3] int64,\n    ln_weight[1536] bf16, ln_bias[1536] bf16,\n    fc1_weight[6144,6144] bf16, fc1_bias[6144] bf16,\n    fc2_weight[3584,6144] bf16, fc2_bias[3584] bf16,\n    eps: float32) -> output[num_merged,3584] bf16\n```\nConstants: `hidden_size C = 1536`, `merge_size = 2`, `hidden_size_expanded E = 6144 = 4*C`,\n`out_hidden_size O = 3584`, `num_merged = num_patches / 4`.\n\nReference pipeline (semantically):\n\n1. **LayerNorm (pre-shuffle), computed in fp32, per input patch over the C=1536 axis**\n   - `x = hidden.float()`\n   - `mean = x.mean(-1, keepdim)`, `var = x.var(-1, keepdim, unbiased=False)` (÷N, N=1536)\n   - `xhat = (x - mean) / sqrt(var + eps)`\n   - `y = xhat * ln_weight.float() + ln_bias.float()`  → cast to **bf16** → `hidden_norm[num_patches,1536]`\n   - Note: generator fills `ln_weight=ones`, `ln_bias=zeros` (identity affine), but we implement\n     the **general** affine using the passed tensors (do not hardcode).\n\n2. **Spatial shuffle (2×2 patch merge), per grid `(t,h,w)`** (h,w even by construction):\n   - `view(t, h/2, 2, w/2, 2, C)` → `permute(0,1,3,2,4,5)` → `reshape(t*(h/2)*(w/2), 4*C=E)`.\n   - Grids concatenated in order → `hidden_shuffled[num_merged, 6144]` bf16.\n   - Decoding: for a grid with row-major patch index `p = ((tt*h + hh)*w + ww)`,\n     with `hh = hm*2 + a`, `ww = wm*2 + b`:\n     - output row (within grid) = `(tt*(h/2) + hm)*(w/2) + wm`\n     - within-row **block** `β = a*2 + b ∈ {0,1,2,3}` occupies columns `[β*1536 : (β+1)*1536]`.\n     - i.e. the 6144-vector = `concat( patch(a=0,b=0), patch(a=0,b=1), patch(a=1,b=0), patch(a=1,b=1) )`.\n   - This is a **pure row-gather / permutation** of already-LayerNormed patches; LayerNorm is\n     per-patch over C so it commutes with the shuffle (LN then gather == gather then LN).\n\n3. **FC1**: `linear(A, fc1_weight, fc1_bias)` = `A @ fc1_weight^T + fc1_bias`,\n   `[num_merged,6144] @ [6144,6144]^T → [num_merged,6144]`. cuBLAS returns **bf16**.\n\n4. **GELU exact (erf)**: `F.gelu(..., approximate='none')` on the bf16 FC1 output → bf16.\n\n5. **FC2**: `linear(gelu, fc2_weight, fc2_bias)` = `[num_merged,6144] @ [3584,6144]^T + bias\n   → [num_merged,3584]` bf16 = **output**.\n\nKey structural fact: `fc1_weight` columns are indexed by the merged 6144 axis, so\n`FC1[m] = Σ_{β=0..3} ( LN(patch_β) @ fc1_weight[:, β*1536:(β+1)*1536]^T ) + bias`. The permutation\nis over **rows/patches**, not columns, so it cannot be folded into the static weight — an explicit\ngather is required.\n\n## 2. Feedback workloads and derived quantities\n\n| uuid# | num_patches | num_merged (M) | num_grids | atol | rtol |\n|------:|------------:|---------------:|----------:|-----:|-----:|\n| 1 | 2048 | 512 | 2 | 0.0022 | 0.05 |\n| 2 | 6400 | 1600 | 1 | 0.0020 | 0.05 |\n| 3 | 1024 | 256 | 4 | 0.0014 | 0.05 |\n| 4 | 512  | 128 | 2 | 0.0021 | 0.05 |\n| 5 | 4096 | 1024 | 4 | 0.0026 | 0.05 |\n\nM ∈ {512, 1600, 256, 128, 1024} — all divisible by 128 except **256/128 boundaries fine**;\n128 and 256 are the small, memory-bound cases. All M divisible by 64; 1600 divisible by 64 not 128\n(1600 = 25*64 = 12.5*128) → a BLOCK_M of 128 leaves a half tile, 64 tiles evenly. Choose GEMM\nblock/masking that handles arbitrary M (mask the M tail) rather than assuming divisibility.\n\nCompute / traffic estimates (per call):\n- FC1 flops = `2*M*E*E = M*75.5e6`; FC2 flops = `2*M*E*O = M*44.0e6`.\n  - M=1600: FC1 ≈ 120.8 GFLOP, FC2 ≈ 70.5 GFLOP, total ≈ 191 GFLOP.\n  - M=128: total ≈ 15.3 GFLOP.\n- Weight bytes (read at least once): fc1 = 6144*6144*2 = 75.5 MB, fc2 = 3584*6144*2 = 44.0 MB;\n  combined ≈ 119.5 MB.\n- Roofline (A800 ~312 TFLOPS bf16, HBM ~1.9 TB/s): weights dominate for **M ≲ ~200**\n  (M=128 ≈ 63 µs weight-read vs ≈ 49 µs compute → memory-bound); M=1600 is compute-bound\n  (~0.6 ms ideal). The two small workloads (M=128, 256) are where the reference's **host-side\n  overhead** (Python per-grid loop with `.item()` syncs + list-append + `torch.cat`) is most\n  visible and where fusion wins the most.\n\n## 3. Where the speedup comes from (baseline inefficiencies)\n\nThe reference `run` is timed as the baseline. Its overheads over an optimal GPU pipeline:\n1. **Host-side grid loop with `grid_thw[i,j].item()`** → up to `3*num_grids` device syncs per call,\n   plus Python-level slicing, `permute/reshape` (materializing copies), list build, and `torch.cat`\n   (an extra full read+write of the shuffled A matrix).\n2. **fp32 LayerNorm materialization**: `hidden.float()` allocates a full fp32 copy of\n   `[num_patches,1536]` and multiple intermediate tensors.\n3. **Separate kernels** for LN, cast, cat, two GEMMs, bias adds, and GELU (each a launch + full\n   HBM round trip of the activations).\n\nOur plan removes host syncs (indices computed from `grid_thw` once), fuses LN+shuffle into a single\nkernel, fuses bias+GELU into the FC1 epilogue and bias into the FC2 epilogue, and keeps activations\nin bf16. The two GEMMs are the irreducible cost; the win is (a) eliminating overhead/extra passes\nand (b) matching cuBLAS GEMM throughput as closely as possible with autotuned Triton.\n\n**Risk:** cuBLAS bf16 GEMM on A800 is strong; for the compute-bound M=1600 case a Triton GEMM may\nland at ~0.8–1.0× of cuBLAS. Overall geomean depends on winning big on small M (overhead-dominated)\nwhile staying near-parity on large M. This is the central tradeoff to validate empirically.\n\n## 4. Triton design space\n\n### 4.1 LayerNorm + shuffle kernel (K1)\nProduces `A_shuffled[num_merged, 6144]` bf16 directly (fused LN + gather), avoiding a separate cast\nand `torch.cat`.\n- **Layout choice A (one program per output patch)** — preferred: grid = `num_merged`. Each program\n  loops β=0..3, gathers source patch row `src(m,β)`, loads its 1536 bf16 values, casts fp32, computes\n  mean/var over 1536, applies `xhat*w+b`, stores 1536 bf16 contiguously at columns `[β*1536:(β+1)*1536]`.\n  Writes are fully contiguous (coalesced 6144-wide row); 4 gathered strided loads. Per-program working\n  set ~1536 fp32 = 6 KB (loop β, don't hold all 4 at once).\n- **Layout choice B (one program per input patch, scatter)**: grid = `num_patches`; each normalizes one\n  patch and scatters 1536 to its computed `(out_row, β)` slot. Same traffic; store is contiguous 1536.\n  Slightly simpler indexing but requires input→dest map. Either is fine; pick A for contiguous 6144\n  stores and simpler reduction.\n- Reduction over C=1536: `1536 = 3*512`. Use `BLOCK_C` covering 1536 with masking, or a fixed 1536\n  `tl.arange` (power-of-two pad to 2048 with mask). Two-pass (mean, then var) or one-pass\n  Welford/`sum`+`sum(x^2)`; a single load into registers + `sum`/`sum(x*x)` is simplest and exact in fp32.\n- eps added inside sqrt: `rstd = 1/sqrt(var + eps)`.\n\n### 4.2 Index construction (avoid host sync)\nTwo options; both are \"plumbing\":\n- **A (single small transfer)**: `grid_thw.to('cpu')` once (4×3 int64) → compute prefix sums and a\n  gather-index int32 tensor `src_idx[num_merged, 4]` (or a base+arithmetic form) on host, upload once.\n  One tiny sync — vastly cheaper than the reference's per-grid `.item()` loop. Simple, robust for\n  `num_grids ∈ {1,2,4}`.\n- **B (fully on-GPU)**: a trivial index kernel reads `grid_thw` on device, does a sequential scan over\n  `num_grids (≤4)`, writes `src_idx`. Zero host sync. More code; keep as a later refinement if the\n  single transfer shows up in timing.\n- `num_merged` and the GEMM launch dims need **no** sync: `num_merged = hidden.shape[0] // 4` is known\n  from tensor shape. Only the permutation mapping depends on `grid_thw` values.\n- Correctness note: implement fully general `(t,h,w)` with h,w even; Σ over grids of `t*(h/2)*(w/2)`\n  = num_patches/4 = num_merged by construction. Do not assume a single grid or square grids.\n\n### 4.3 FC1 GEMM (K2): `A[M,6144] @ fc1_weight[6144,6144]^T + bias`, GELU epilogue\n- Standard tiled bf16 GEMM, **fp32 accumulator** (matches cuBLAS accumulation).\n- Weight is row-major `[N=out, K=in]`; accessing `W[n, k]` gives K contiguous along the K reduction —\n  natural for `tl.dot(a[BM,BK], w[BK,BN])` after loading `W` tile as `[BK,BN]` via transpose indexing.\n- Epilogue: `acc += fc1_bias[n]`; **match reference rounding**: reference rounds FC1 to bf16 *before*\n  GELU (cuBLAS bf16 output), so cast `acc` (with bias) to bf16, then apply GELU, then keep bf16 for FC2\n  input. (Applying GELU on fp32 then rounding differs by < bf16 ulp; will test both, prefer the\n  reference-matching order to be safe against the tight atol.)\n- **GELU exact**: `0.5*x*(1 + erf(x * 0.70710678118654752440))` using `tl.math.erf` (or libdevice\n  erf). **Do not** use tanh approximation unless testing shows it stays within atol — erf is the\n  reference and safer given atol as low as 0.0014.\n- Autotune space: `BLOCK_M ∈ {64,128}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32,64}`,\n  `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {3,4,5}`, `GROUP_M ∈ {4,8}` (L2 grouping / swizzle).\n- **Occupancy for small M**: M=128 with BLOCK_M=128 → 1 M-tile; N=6144/BLOCK_N tiles. With BLOCK_N=128\n  → 48 programs on 108 SMs (under-utilized). Mitigations: smaller BLOCK_M/BLOCK_N to raise tile count,\n  or **split-K** (2–4 way) to fill SMs (needs fp32 atomic add or a second reduction pass). Consider a\n  separate small-M config selected by M.\n\n### 4.4 FC2 GEMM (K3): `gelu[M,6144] @ fc2_weight[3584,6144]^T + bias`\n- Same GEMM template, `K=6144`, `N=3584`, bias epilogue only, output bf16.\n- `3584 = 28*128 = 14*256` → divisible by 128 and 256; N tiling clean.\n- Same autotune space; N smaller so fewer N-tiles → split-K more relevant for small M.\n\n### 4.5 Fusion boundaries\n- Cannot fuse FC1 and FC2 into one kernel (GELU nonlinearity + different K/N reductions). Keep 3\n  kernels: K1 (LN+shuffle), K2 (FC1+bias+GELU), K3 (FC2+bias).\n- Optional later: skip materializing `A_shuffled` and gather normalized rows inside the FC1 prologue.\n  Blocked by LN needing the whole 1536 row to compute mean/var (a BLOCK_K < 1536 tile lacks the full\n  row), and 1536 not being a power of two. Simpler and nearly-free to materialize A in K1 first\n  (extra ≈ M*6144*2 bytes write+read; ≈ 19.6 MB at M=1600 vs 191 GFLOP — negligible). Prefer\n  materialize-then-GEMM.\n\n## 5. Numerical correctness & risks\n\n1. **LayerNorm precision**: accumulate mean/var in fp32, `unbiased=False` (÷1536), affine in fp32,\n   cast output to bf16 — exactly mirrors the reference. Risk: using bf16 accumulation would blow atol.\n2. **GEMM accumulation**: fp32 accumulator required to match cuBLAS. bf16 inputs.\n3. **GELU form**: exact erf. tanh-approx GELU differs by up to ~3e-4 in activation; after the FC2 sum\n   over 6144 terms it could approach the atol (0.0014–0.0026). Use erf; keep tanh only as a tunable to\n   test if it ever helps speed without failing tolerance.\n4. **Intermediate rounding order (FC1→GELU)**: reference rounds to bf16 before GELU. Replicate to avoid\n   a systematic bias near the tight atol.\n5. **Output dtype**: bf16 store with round-to-nearest (Triton default).\n6. **Value magnitudes**: LN output ~unit variance; FC1/FC2 outputs O(1) (weights scaled by\n   1/sqrt(6144), bias N(0,1)). atol ~0.002 on O(1) values ≈ 0.2% absolute; bf16 has ~0.4% relative\n   precision but fp32-accumulated GEMM keeps error well inside the combined `atol + rtol*|ref|`\n   (rtol 0.05 dominates for |ref|≳0.04). Expect comfortable pass with fp32 accum + erf.\n7. **Determinism**: no split-K atomics in the correctness-first candidate (atomic fp32 add reorders\n   sums → small nondeterminism); if split-K is adopted for speed, re-verify tolerance.\n8. **Edge/general handling**: arbitrary `num_grids ∈ {1,2,4}`, arbitrary even (h,w), t≥1; M-tail masking\n   in GEMM (M not always a multiple of BLOCK_M, e.g. 1600 vs 128).\n\n## 6. Validation strategy\n\nConstraints: I cannot run CUDA, a profiler, or any harness directly; the **only** correctness+timing\nsignal is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` over the fixed 5 workloads (one candidate =\nall 5). Budget: 100 candidate evals; token soft/hard 1.0M/1.2M.\n\nPlan:\n- **c001 — correctness-first, conservative**: K1 LN+shuffle (per-output-patch, fp32 LN), K2 FC1 GEMM\n  (moderate config e.g. BM128/BN128/BK64, warps8, stages3, fp32 accum, bias, erf GELU with\n  bf16-before-GELU rounding), K3 FC2 GEMM (bias). Host-side index build (option A). Evaluate; confirm\n  all 5 pass and record per-workload speedup + geomean as the anchor.\n- **Iterate one dimension per candidate** (each a new immutable ID):\n  - GEMM autotune configs (block sizes, stages, warps, GROUP_M/swizzle).\n  - Small-M path (split-K or smaller blocks) for M=128/256; verify tolerance still holds after any\n    atomic-based split-K.\n  - K1 layout / vectorization tweaks; on-GPU index kernel (option B) if the single transfer shows cost.\n  - Epilogue rounding-order A/B test; erf vs tanh (only if within tolerance).\n- **Guardrails**: never regress a passing candidate's correctness for speed; a Triton failure is\n  invalid (no Torch fallback). Track cumulative eval count; stop when geomean converges, then write\n  `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Never run `final` without operator approval.\n- Record for every candidate in `candidates.jsonl`: parent, source hash, hypothesis, validation,\n  per-workload result, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage.\n\n## 7. Skill usage\n\n- `KernelWiki`: **not applicable** — Blackwell/Hopper only; target is A800/sm_80 (Ampere).\n- `ncu-report-skill`: **not applicable / not permitted** — B200/sm_100 and requires running a profiler,\n  which the isolation rules forbid.\n- No other external knowledge sources are permitted; optimization relies on standard Ampere Triton\n  GEMM practice and the evaluator feedback loop.\n\n## 8. Open questions to resolve empirically via the evaluator\n\n1. Does Triton FC1/FC2 reach cuBLAS parity at M=1600 (compute-bound)? If not, how large is the deficit\n   vs the overhead win on small M in the geomean?\n2. Is the single `grid_thw` host transfer negligible, or worth the on-GPU index kernel?\n3. Best small-M strategy (split-K vs small blocks) and whether split-K stays within atol.\n4. Does the bf16-before-GELU rounding order matter for tolerance vs a pure-fp32 epilogue?\n","numLines":224,"startLine":1,"totalLines":224}}}
